<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>3.10. Trigonometric Integrals</title>

    <alert>
        MTH 401
    </alert>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Trig integrals are difficult (if not impossible) to solve when restricted to real numbers. 
        But in complex land, they aren't nearly as terrible. It is wise to recall 
        <term>Euler's formula</term> (and its rearrangements)
        <md>
          <mrow> e^{i\theta} = \cos(\theta)+i\sin(\theta), ~~~~~ \amp ~~~~~ \cos(\theta) = \frac12\left(e^{i\theta}+e^{-i\theta}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow> e^{-i\theta} = \cos(\theta)-i\sin(\theta), ~~~~~ \amp ~~~~~ \sin(\theta) = \frac1{2i}\left(e^{i\theta}-e^{-i\theta}\right)</mrow>
        </md>   
       
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <remark>
      <p>
         Specifically on the unit circle we have <m>{z = e^{i\theta}}</m> where <m>{0\le \theta \le 2\pi}</m>, 
        and thus <m>{\frac1z = e^{-i\theta}}</m>. Therefore we can further rewrite Euler's formula:
        <md>
          <mrow> \cos(\theta)  \amp = \frac{e^{i\theta}+e^{-i\theta}}2 = \frac12\left(z+\frac1z\right)</mrow>
          <mrow> \sin(\theta)  \amp = \frac{e^{i\theta}-e^{-i\theta}}{2i} = \frac1{2i}\left(z-\frac1z\right)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </remark>

    <exercise workspace="5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use residue calculus to evaluate
                <md>
                    \int_0^{2\pi}\frac{1}{2+\cos\theta}~d\theta.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use residue calculus to evaluate
                <md>
                    \int_0^{2\pi}\frac{1}{9\sin^2\theta + 16\cos^2\theta}~d\theta.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>